    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property",
    "problem": "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]).$",
    "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]) & =T(4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=4T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
